  "response": "## Pick: `dateLabel()` at src/date.ts:39–42\n\n**Receiver:** `d` (declared at L40 as `new Date()`).\n\n**Methods that disagree on that same receiver, all at L41:**\n- `d.getUTCFullYear()` — UTC family\n- `d.getMonth()` — local family\n- `d.getDate()` — local family\n\nThe crimes finding (`crime_00003`) collapses receivers named `d` across the file, so its line pair (9, 41) bridges two functions. The actual single-receiver mix lives only in `dateLabel()` — `todayKey()` at L7–10 is already consistently UTC.\n\n**Refactor: commit to the UTC family.**\n\nReasoning:\n- The neighbouring `todayKey()` already uses UTC for a date key, so picking UTC here keeps the file's storage/identity-style outputs in one time domain.\n- `prettyDueDate()` is already the local-display boundary for this module — that's where local methods belong.\n- The current code also drops zero-padding and the 0-indexed-month correction (separate `date_string_concat` finding `crime_00024`); fixing the family lets us fix the format in one pass.\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(): string {\n  return new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10); // YYYY-MM-DD, UTC\n}\n```\n\nIf you want to keep the explicit field-by-field shape (e.g. because callers expect non-padded segments later), the minimal in-place fix is to swap the two local reads for their UTC siblings on the same `d`:\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(): string {\n  const d = new Date();\n  const y = d.getUTCFullYear();\n  const m = String(d.getUTCMonth() + 1).padStart(2, \"0\");\n  const day = String(d.getUTCDate()).padStart(2, \"0\");\n  return `${y}-${m}-${day}`;\n}\n```\n\nEither form leaves one time domain per receiver and clears `crime_00003` (and the related `crime_00024` concat finding) in the same edit.",
